[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Read the reports and it is three. The
merge rule ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) matched on subject, and every
report about a wrong date has a subject that sounds the same.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the first row. The other two keep coming in after it ships.
</Callout>

## What is in #4471

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

The export repeats some reports across its pages; each id is counted once. *Mistagged* counts the
reports intake tagged as something else: intake guesses from the reporter's browser, the cause
depends on the tenant.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Day-first tenants get day and month
swapped: "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July." Every tenant on file, every channel. This is the
fix already queued.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** People at day-first tenants type
what the hint says, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way. Same swap, different bug:
only reports through the form, only tenants on booking-v3. Fixing the parser does not touch it; the
hint has to follow the tenant's locale.

**`one-day`: the date moves by exactly one day.** "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday
11 May." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting." No month or day
is swapped, so neither fix above reaches it. It reads like a timezone conversion on the date. The
desk already noted these (`desk-notes.json`), and the #4471 page footnotes them under `parser`.

## For the desk

- When a report gives both dates, check them: a swap and a one-day move are not the same bug.
- Place a report by its tenant and channel, not by the intake tag.
- Reopen #4471 as the `parser` ticket only; the other two have their own.

